&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="sq"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-bg" src="All_of_a_Sudden.jpg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-fg" src="All_of_a_Sudden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
Hamaguchi returns with his stage-play-disguised-as-a-film conversational epic. It&amp;rsquo;s about the paradox of running a humane care home under capitalism. It&amp;rsquo;s about unexpected friendships. It&amp;rsquo;s about making a play (of course). It&amp;rsquo;s about facing your mortality. It&amp;rsquo;s about work life balance. It&amp;rsquo;s about how we take care of each other. Halfway through the movie there is a half-hour long lecture about capitalism. It&amp;rsquo;s 200 minutes of nonstop dialog. No doubt my favorite one at BIFF. But I knew that before I went in.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Lucia's Tab</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/lucia-tab/</link><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/lucia-tab/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="in-which-i-keep-track-of-the-tab-between-me-and-ana-lucia-de-santos-medina"&gt;in which i keep track of the tab between me and ana lucia de santos medina&lt;/h1&gt;
