&lt;li&gt;(-) disgusting pizza&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(+) friendship&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</description></item><item><title>Two Books about AI</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_1/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_1/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="klara-and-the-sun"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/54120408"&gt;Klara and the Sun&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="byline"&gt;Kazuo Ishiguro · 2021&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="sq"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-bg" src="Klara_and_the_Sun.png" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-fg" src="Klara_and_the_Sun.png" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A world where robots with the mind of a child and yet superhuman abilities are being sold as Artificial Friends (AFs) for children. I love that you could not really tell what Ishiguro&amp;rsquo;s message is. Most of the story revolves around how an AF, Klara, interprets and acts in the world as she spends her time with her child companion. The choice that the AFs are fully equipped top-of-the-spec shells and yet near zero understanding of the human world makes it less a story about robots but one about humans. We grow up and move on. We disappoint each other. We accept this as the necessary human condition. But to Klara, all she had left in the end was the benevolent Sun.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Two Excruciating Stories</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_2/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_2/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="husbands"&gt;&lt;a href="https://letterboxd.com/film/husbands/"&gt;Husbands&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="sq"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-bg" src="Husbands.jpg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-fg" src="Husbands.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="byline"&gt;John Cassavetes · 1970&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I saw Husbands by John Cassavetes at Bergen Filmklubb. Excruciating is the only word I have to describe the experience. You follow three middle-aged white collar workers in New York getting shitfaced after attending a friend’s funeral. Recognizing their mortality, they decide to go on the ultimate bro’s trip. Hungover basketball. Getting shitfaced and forcing old people to sing for them. Beating up the wife and terrorizing the family. Trying to throw up but your bro is talking too much. Flying to London on a whim with no luggage. Trying to hook up but all you are doing is either crying in bed together or attempting (roleplaying?) rape. The ultimate frat bro movie but with middle-aged guys.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Tobias's Tab</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/tobias-tab/</link><pubDate>Sat, 25 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/tobias-tab/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="in-which-i-keep-track-of-how-much-money-i-owe-mr-tobias-eiksund"&gt;in which i keep track of how much money i owe mr. tobias eiksund&lt;/h1&gt;
